 "overall": {
  "verdict_label": "Conclusion: move the brand name into the warm zone and keep A's gaze cue, then test recall",
  "verdict_text": "In both designs viewers notice the tagline first, then the pack, the face, the logo and last the brand name banner, and that order does not change from the first 3 to the first 10 seconds. The brand banner is the weak spot: its VAI is 14.3 in design A and 8.3 in design B. Design A reaches the logo and banner more often; design B holds the face better. Without a recall result this is a diagnosis, not a winner.",
  "summary": "All scores are RealEye VAI (0–100) and its three measures: Reach (how many viewers looked), Speed (how soon) and Hold (how long attention stayed).",
  "kpis": [
   {
    "label": "Viewing order, both designs",
    "value": "Tagline → Pack → Face → Logo → Banner",
    "note": "same in the first 3, 5 and 10 s"
   },
   {
    "label": "Brand banner VAI, first 10 s",
    "value": "A 14.3 · B 8.3",
    "note": "Reach 27% vs 16%"
   },
   {
    "label": "Face VAI, first 10 s",
    "value": "A 54.2 · B 58.4",
    "note": "direct gaze holds the face"
   },
   {
    "label": "Tagline VAI, women minus men",
    "value": "A +7 · B +10",
    "note": "+16 / +12 in the first 3 s"
   }
  ],
  "findings": [
   {
    "tag": "strength",
    "title": "Tagline and pack lead, in every window",
    "text": "Tagline VAI is 57.5 (A) and 57.6 (B) in the first 3 s with Reach near 80%. The pack follows (VAI 42.2 and 41.0) and catches up by 10 s (61.2 and 61.8). This part of the layout works."
   },
   {
    "tag": "risk",
    "title": "The brand name banner is the weak spot",
    "text": "Banner VAI is 3.6 → 7.5 → 14.3 in A and 1.2 → 3.6 → 8.3 in B (first 3, 5 and 10 s). Reach is 27% and 16% and Hold is about 1: few viewers get there, late, and only briefly. The heatmaps show the bottom band cold in every audience."
   },
   {
    "tag": "risk",
    "title": "Direct gaze helps the face and costs the brand",
    "text": "In B the face scores higher (VAI 58.4 vs 54.2, Hold 17.0 vs 12.8), while the logo falls from 28.0 to 23.9 and the banner from 14.3 to 8.3. Gaze direction is the likely cause but the designs differ in more than the pose."
   },
   {
    "tag": "strength",
    "title": "Women get to the claim first",
    "text": "Women score higher on the tagline than men in every window and in both designs: VAI +15.6 and +12.1 in the first 3 s, +7.2 and +10.4 in the first 10 s (A and B). In A, men reach the face and pack earlier."
   },
   {
    "tag": "weak-evidence",
    "title": "Age groups other than 18–24 are small",
    "text": "18–24 viewers (82 and 83) behave like the overall sample. Groups of 25–34 (28 and 31) and 35+ (7 and 14) are too small to separate real differences from noise."
   }
  ],
  "conclusions": [
   "The tagline is the entry point and the pack the anchor: keep both where they are.",
   "The brand name is only reliably seen on the pack label. The bottom banner adds little: move the brand name into the tagline-to-pack column, or raise and enlarge the banner.",
   "Use design A's profile gaze as the base for brand visibility; test a direct-gaze face with the brand name on the eye line.",
   "Women reach the claim sooner than men in both designs: do not move or shrink the tagline without retesting.",
   "VAI shows attention, not understanding: confirm the changes with an unaided brand and message recall test."
  ]
 },
